WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.812 --> 00:00:08.030
I like to do it the old
school way, with respect.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:11.160
I can take things and put them
together and take them apart

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.070
and make them better and
put them back together.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.210
I respect the craft,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.789
so I don't want to minimize and disrespect

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:19.710
the value of each step of the craft

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.186
and do by machine what
should be done by hand.

00:00:23.186 --> 00:00:25.470
I've made a career out of
making colorful clothes.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.080
Like I'm a grown man that
makes colorful clothes.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.010
These clothes look so playful and easy,

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.500
but it's such an intricate process.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.123
And I want people to know
that this is what it is.

00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:34.956
This is luxury.

00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:37.110
This is luxury athleisure.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:41.750
For many generations,
African-American designers

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.985
have not been included
within the conversation

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:47.353
of high fashion or
considered high fashion.

00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:52.300
In recent years, we see that
emergence and that movement

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.120
to really kind of put Black
designers on that same level

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.550
that we have cared for
European designers in that way.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.210
With Black designers, their
work is just as critical,

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.740
just as artistic as any
European or white designer,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.283
or non-Black designer.

00:01:11.135 --> 00:01:15.650
All the Black fashion is
inspired by movements,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.160
by the simplicity of what I do,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.780
by how clean my stuff is,

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.143
by my ability to just put
my flag in luxury and say,

00:01:22.143 --> 00:01:24.547
"I'm a Black designer
and I do luxury too."

00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:33.300
Hello. My name is Fresh.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.700
I'm the creator, I'm the
CEO, creative director

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.060
for luxury label, Rich Fresh.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.890
We specialize in luxury
athleisure and tailored garments.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.400
I'm a tailor by trade.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.600
I'm a designer by choice.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.240
We're downtown in the Garment District,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.262
close proximity to where everything is.

00:01:57.262 --> 00:01:59.400
There's just an energy that's downtown.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.654
There's a creative energy.

00:02:00.654 --> 00:02:02.980
We knew that we could
find the type of factory

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.643
we needed to build.

00:02:04.643 --> 00:02:06.351
You gotta think about a person

00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:09.130
that was homeless at one point,

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.880
didn't really have any money coming in.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.075
The fact that this person was relentless

00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:16.533
and just determined to succeed

00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:19.750
and not let rock bottom
define him as an individual.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.394
I look like Puff Daddy.

00:02:21.394 --> 00:02:22.850
I always say I'm from Memphis,

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.230
but I was born in Germany.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.260
You know, my parents were both military.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.397
They were in the army.

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:30.856
A lot of my early
memories are like Germany.

00:02:30.856 --> 00:02:33.230
So like the colors on the clothes

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.790
that I used to see people wearing,

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:36.520
the font was different.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.170
The advertisements were different.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.417
The architecture was different.

00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:41.508
Everything was just different in Germany

00:02:41.508 --> 00:02:42.980
than it was in the US.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.580
Subconsciously, it just
got planted there somewhere

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.390
and I always kind of revert back to,

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.420
this doesn't feel like
something you see in America.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.210
This feels like something you could see

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.293
in Germany or abroad.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.930
Once we moved to the States,
my first grade was normal,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.250
but I was bored.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.780
I was bored out of my skull.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.560
The second grade, they put
me in this advanced class

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.188
with second graders and third graders

00:03:05.188 --> 00:03:06.350
who were really performing

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.320
on like a fifth and sixth grade level.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.225
And there was this girl
who I had a crush on.

00:03:11.225 --> 00:03:16.033
Mind you, I'm one of the
smartest kids at the school

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:17.526
and math is my thing.

00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:22.198
So the teacher asked if I would tutor her.

00:03:22.198 --> 00:03:26.060
And then I had this
moment where I heard God,

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.160
and I have this thing
from time to time where

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.800
I hear the voice of reason that said,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.740
"You're doing this all wrong."

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.030
She has a higher purpose.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.005
She has information that
you need to acquire.

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:40.140
So the first day of tutoring,
as soon as we sat down,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.070
I just looked the her right in the face.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.880
I'm like, "you need to know math.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.730
I'm very, very, very good at this.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:45.579
I got you.

00:03:45.579 --> 00:03:47.089
I suck at girls.

00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:49.014
How about we do an exchange?

00:03:49.014 --> 00:03:50.110
I'll teach you this.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.330
You teach me that."

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.157
She was like, "honestly,
it's the way you dress.

00:03:53.157 --> 00:03:55.439
I think if you dressed
different, girls would like you.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:57.167
You dress like a kid."

00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:59.540
I said, "What's it called?"

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:00.750
She said, "I think it's called fashion."

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.840
I said, "Hm. Well, all right.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.362
Let me see what this fashion thing does."

00:04:06.362 --> 00:04:07.620
Sup?
Appreciate it.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.110
First thing I do is I
meet with the client.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.050
Ellis.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.470
All right, man. All right.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.223
I take their measurements,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:18.383
39.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.170
44 and three-quarters.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.562
27 and a half.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.440
22.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.170
Track jacket length at a 26 and a half.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.660
Skin ankle at an 11 and a half.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.410
And then we discuss colors, concepts.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:34.410
Navy, black kinda goes with that, yeah.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.962
And that olive is a
popping color nowadays too.

00:04:36.962 --> 00:04:39.070
Okay, okay.
Yeah, I like that.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.890
Then I'll put together a digital mock up

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.360
of the tracksuit with
all the various colors

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.830
that I'm gonna use for the
base, for the top stripe,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.580
the bottom stripe, the piping,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.163
all the different layers
of the track suit.

00:04:52.176 --> 00:04:54.220
Then I'm gonna send that to Michelle

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.860
who's my production manager.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.781
She takes everything from
concept to production.

00:04:59.781 --> 00:05:03.962
She is responsible for
delegating who gets what.

00:05:03.962 --> 00:05:05.970
Like I've got a number of jacket tailors.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.205
I've got a number of pant tailors.

00:05:07.205 --> 00:05:09.450
I've got a number of hand finishers

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.393
who are going to complete the process.

00:05:13.476 --> 00:05:14.776
I show a six .

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.120
Seven and three-quarters.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.030
The first step of the
production cycle is the pattern.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.425
You know, you can't make any
garments without a pattern.

00:05:22.425 --> 00:05:25.127
So she's gonna take it
to our pattern maker

00:05:25.127 --> 00:05:27.239
and show him all the various measurements.

00:05:27.239 --> 00:05:29.080
He's gonna build out the pattern,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:32.091
draw every piece out, cut every piece.

00:05:32.091 --> 00:05:36.590
It's an old school process
of measurements, marks,

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:39.090
measurements, marks, measurements, marks,

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.430
drawing lines, drawing
lines, drawing lines,

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.973
drawing lines, drawing
lines, drawing lines.

00:05:43.973 --> 00:05:45.420
Cutting.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:46.350
That's your block.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.039
That's your pattern.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:50.630
So the pattern has to get
transferred to the garment

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.784
and oftentimes it'll just get laid on top.

00:05:52.784 --> 00:05:55.010
Tailors are so precise, they
don't even need to pin it.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:58.965
And then from there you
got to cut out the fabric.

00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:02.570
It's a special grade of
neoprene, more water repellent.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.910
It's a little bit more dense.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.420
It's a lighter weight.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.530
It absorbs colors really well.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.860
And it has a nice sheen.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.160
It looks really luxurious.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.497
My senior year in high school, I was like,

00:06:14.497 --> 00:06:16.290
I'm gonna take home ec,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.190
just to see if there's
anything else I can learn.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.240
My teacher was out.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.944
We had another teacher
who came in to instruct,

00:06:22.944 --> 00:06:24.490
but they couldn't sew,

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.246
so I volunteered to be that person

00:06:26.246 --> 00:06:28.427
because I could sew better
than anyone else in the class.

00:06:28.427 --> 00:06:30.210
My kids would call me Ralph.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.560
They would call me Tommy,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.690
because, you know, it was Tommy Hilfiger.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:33.730
It was Ralph Lauren.

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.150
My brother Chase, he
used to dress real rough;

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.270
camouflage pants, oversized.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.080
And I always saw how he got dealt with.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.460
And so one day I was like, "Hey, bro.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.572
We gotta switch that up."

00:06:42.572 --> 00:06:45.365
The first moment I knew there
was something different,

00:06:45.365 --> 00:06:47.740
like other than the rest of the kids

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.762
that were playing basketball and tennis

00:06:49.762 --> 00:06:52.260
and baseball in the neighborhood was,

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.270
this dude made a pair of moccasins

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.140
and then took them to school
and was like selling them

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.358
to kids and actually taking orders.

00:06:58.358 --> 00:07:00.540
So I started like dressing him.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:01.373
You know, styling him.

00:07:01.373 --> 00:07:03.860
Like the whole thing, from head to toe.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.340
You're gonna get your hair
cut when I get my hair cut.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.670
We're gonna do this.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:07.940
Boom, boom, boom.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.460
And so I saw his image shift.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.323
He was perceived differently.

00:07:12.727 --> 00:07:17.700
In regarding this new era that we're in,

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.140
this new chapter where you
have these Black designers

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:26.556
really inserting themselves
into the luxury space

00:07:26.556 --> 00:07:30.098
is quite a fascinating dichotomy.

00:07:30.098 --> 00:07:32.560
I applaud it.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.604
They're really kind of
building on the shoulders

00:07:36.604 --> 00:07:39.910
of African American
designers who couldn't,

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:42.940
and couldn't really stay
there for a long time

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:44.818
and have long standing power.

00:07:44.818 --> 00:07:46.672
Zelda Wynn Valdes,

00:07:46.672 --> 00:07:50.570
very much known in the
Harlem realm of dressmaking

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.287
and in New York fashion,
specifically uptown,

00:07:53.287 --> 00:07:56.540
she was credited with producing,

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.535
manufacturing the Playboy costume.

00:07:58.535 --> 00:08:01.850
Kind of a heralding figure within fashion,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.480
and for African Americans

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:06.364
because really she was a
part of several organizations

00:08:06.364 --> 00:08:08.815
that really mobilized around the education

00:08:08.815 --> 00:08:12.010
of African Americans within dressmaking

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.540
and within the garment industry

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.920
and giving them new opportunities

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.799
that most people weren't even thinking.

00:08:17.799 --> 00:08:21.810
You have Stephen Burrows in the 1970s

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.980
who rose to fame with one of the most

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.329
notorious fashion events
on this side of the pond,

00:08:26.329 --> 00:08:28.720
the Battle of Versailles in 1973

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.190
that took place at the
Palace of Versailles,

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:32.470
where you had the American designers

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:34.070
pitted against the French designers.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.783
And Stephen Burrows was the
only Black designer present.

00:08:36.783 --> 00:08:40.173
A lot of things happened
within his journey.

00:08:40.173 --> 00:08:42.500
Some things that were out of his control.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.324
You have to chalk it up
to the system of fashion

00:08:46.324 --> 00:08:49.162
that didn't allow him the standing power

00:08:49.162 --> 00:08:53.373
that someone like Ralph Lauren had.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.350
Everything is custom.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.108
Everything is so specific.

00:08:59.108 --> 00:09:01.308
We don't have an assembly line system.

00:09:01.308 --> 00:09:05.796
When it touches the tailor,
it finishes with that tailor.

00:09:05.796 --> 00:09:09.030
The pattern maker is
also cutting the fabric.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.980
So he's gonna cut the block
that's gonna be the trouser.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.090
He's gonna cut the block
that's gonna be the jacket.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.460
There are a number of
steps that go into it.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.680
We've got to assemble the sleeve panel.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.551
This sleeve is a two panel sleeve.

00:09:22.551 --> 00:09:24.330
So this is two different pieces

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:25.771
that have to be sewn together.

00:09:25.771 --> 00:09:28.070
It needs a top stitch just to reinforce

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:29.975
the outer seam on the sleeve.

00:09:29.975 --> 00:09:33.070
We've got a chest panel
which takes into account

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:34.380
each of the track stripes.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.161
We've got a pocket that's
cut into the lower panel.

00:09:37.161 --> 00:09:39.210
On each side, but again,

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.642
this pocket is cut through the stripes.

00:09:40.642 --> 00:09:41.990
They've got to put each

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.158
of these pieces together
very specifically.

00:09:44.158 --> 00:09:47.880
We've got our collar
assembly, the outer collar.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.794
We got our inner collar assembly.

00:09:49.794 --> 00:09:52.770
The back has to be assembled,

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.607
so, you know, all of the
stripes have to be built into

00:09:55.607 --> 00:09:58.263
the back panel as well.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:01.260
There, the zipper assembly

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:03.190
with the zipper facing is built in.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:04.493
All the final touches

00:10:04.493 --> 00:10:07.113
before it makes its way to hand finishing.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.250
I got married young.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:13.290
I got divorced young.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:14.170
I got married when I was 20.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:15.644
So now I'm 21.

00:10:15.644 --> 00:10:18.590
I'm starting this new
business doing alterations,

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.560
'cause it's the only thing
I really know how to do.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.920
And I figured, you know,
the goal is to get volume,

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.007
and I need to get money.

00:10:24.007 --> 00:10:27.050
I started my first business
maybe six months later

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:29.490
doing alterations for dry cleaners.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:30.760
Two years.

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:32.130
I had it and then I closed it,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.479
which was a traumatic experience.

00:10:33.479 --> 00:10:34.980
But then I noticed a cool thing.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:36.660
People started calling me,

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.300
not for the cleaning,
but for the tailoring.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.140
They were like, "Hey man, sorry
to hear about your cleaners,

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.120
but can you still alter this suit?

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.560
I really loved the way you
did this, that and the other."

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:46.393
So I was like,

00:10:46.393 --> 00:10:47.470
"Mm, I'm gonna position myself

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.667
as a concierge tailor and I'm
gonna just travel to people."

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.780
Before I got divorced, I had a child.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:55.940
So right when my daughter
was like six months old,

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:57.220
me and her mom split.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.212
That was the first time I
just thought about like,

00:10:59.212 --> 00:11:01.474
I don't have to do this
thing here in Memphis.

00:11:01.474 --> 00:11:04.310
Life is gonna change dramatically.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.290
I don't have to do this thing
where I've been doing it.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.990
You know, I just got tired
of being broke in Memphis.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.753
Then my brother lost his job in Nashville.

00:11:11.753 --> 00:11:14.231
So I was like, "Bro, just
come back to Memphis.

00:11:14.231 --> 00:11:16.070
We'll figure out what it is."

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.590
We would talk about dreams and big things,

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.810
and like what could one day be possible.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.650
We just came to the realization
that it was where we were.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:26.460
Didn't matter how progressive we moved.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.070
The environment was not gonna lend itself

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:29.592
to the success that we deserved.

00:11:29.592 --> 00:11:31.163
We had to be somewhere else.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.516
Let's go to the west coast.

00:11:33.516 --> 00:11:35.380
We hopped in the car and we just drove

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:36.960
for three days across the country.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.490
Landed in San Diego,
started up our old business,

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:41.432
doing alterations for dry cleaners.

00:11:41.432 --> 00:11:44.040
And then, you know, disaster struck.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.180
Our parents got divorced right
when my daughter was born.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.267
So up to that point, my dad had
never even seen my daughter.

00:11:48.267 --> 00:11:50.570
And then out of nowhere we get a call

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:54.233
that he's in the hospital and
it's bad, and he dies at 52.

00:11:56.091 --> 00:11:57.700
Just out of nowhere.

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:59.000
I ended up losing all my accounts

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.100
with the dry cleaning stuff.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.390
Like my brain wasn't there.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:03.330
I didn't care about any of that anymore.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.777
So one day we decided to just
take a drive to Los Angeles.

00:12:06.777 --> 00:12:08.850
And I had a client, a rich client.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.363
He said he needed a bunch of suits made.

00:12:10.363 --> 00:12:13.222
I was like, you talking my language, Jack.

00:12:13.222 --> 00:12:15.922
I used my money to front the cost

00:12:15.922 --> 00:12:18.811
to produce all these suits,
which is all of the money.

00:12:18.811 --> 00:12:20.688
That payment never got made.

00:12:20.688 --> 00:12:23.672
So here I am now, I
ain't got no more money.

00:12:23.672 --> 00:12:26.831
I went from having
$8,000 just the other day

00:12:26.831 --> 00:12:29.072
to now I got like 200 bucks.

00:12:29.072 --> 00:12:33.164
I look up and now I'm homeless.

00:12:33.164 --> 00:12:34.820
I hear God.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:38.290
And I hear this voice
that says, "You're so mad.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:39.280
You're just so mad.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.150
I'm just really disappointed
in how you've chosen

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.544
to make this man your God and not me.

00:12:43.544 --> 00:12:46.330
You go to this dude every
day asking for this money.

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:48.451
You ain't came to me not once.

00:12:48.451 --> 00:12:50.068
He cannot save you.

00:12:50.068 --> 00:12:51.416
I can save you.

00:12:51.416 --> 00:12:53.080
You need to talk to me."

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:54.460
And I listened to God.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:55.740
I don't want to be this guy Rich,

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.550
who has just chased the money

00:12:57.550 --> 00:12:59.880
but he lost his soul in the process.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.100
I want to be something different.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:02.930
So I'm gonna change my name to Fresh.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:03.763
I'm gonna chase that.

00:13:03.763 --> 00:13:04.640
The money's gonna come,

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.621
but let me just be focused on the business

00:13:06.621 --> 00:13:08.562
and being different.

00:13:08.562 --> 00:13:11.140
I left the shelter with $300.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:14.630
And I told God, I'm gonna
take this measly ass $300

00:13:15.639 --> 00:13:18.318
and I'm gonna turn this into
a million dollars in a year.

00:13:18.318 --> 00:13:21.689
The real kicker was that tracksuit.

00:13:21.689 --> 00:13:25.983
When he dropped the tracksuit,
I was like, that was it.

00:13:25.983 --> 00:13:27.083
That was game over.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.400
I noticed this lady with
a color block sweater on

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:31.957
and I just thought about how cool it was

00:13:31.957 --> 00:13:35.285
that whoever designed that
sweater could design it one way,

00:13:35.285 --> 00:13:38.590
but change the colors and manipulate it

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.048
to create 10,000 different
variations of that one sweater.

00:13:42.048 --> 00:13:45.400
I thought about what I
could create like that.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:49.550
And I came up with this pant
that had prominent stripes.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:50.620
You know, they were like trousers,

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.160
but there was some color blocking

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:53.680
and I could design it one way

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.893
and make it in a bunch
of different colors,

00:13:55.893 --> 00:13:58.129
and the material would
be super comfortable.

00:13:58.129 --> 00:14:00.530
Then I followed up with a jacket.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:02.613
Just kinda needed a
jacket to wear with it,

00:14:02.613 --> 00:14:04.192
and I like retro style.

00:14:04.192 --> 00:14:06.630
He did some suits for Draymond Green

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:08.701
and some other ballplayers
like right out the gate.

00:14:08.701 --> 00:14:10.820
He was doing some really dope suits.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.214
Rich Fresh made the suit.

00:14:12.214 --> 00:14:14.650
He go by Rich Freshy from LA.

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:17.984
Well he not from LA, but he
lives and works out of LA.

00:14:17.984 --> 00:14:19.453
He's really good.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.160
A Rich Fresh garment is
not a Rich Fresh garment

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.740
without two indicating factors.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:29.190
One is this yellow tab right
on the side of this garment.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.200
There's a yellow grosgrain tab.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:33.480
It's very specific.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.420
It's on the side and every single

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.198
Rich Fresh garment in existence,

00:14:38.198 --> 00:14:42.790
on the trousers as well as the jacket.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:45.200
On every garment, whether
it's a suit jacket,

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.660
whether it's a robe, I don't care.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.250
And then the second thing,

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.560
there's always Rich Fresh,
made in Los Angeles,

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.261
because it is in fact made in Los Angeles

00:14:55.261 --> 00:14:57.160
and people should be aware of it.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:14:59.230
And then it goes from there

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.616
to almost the last step
in the production process.

00:15:01.616 --> 00:15:03.578
You've got the waistband.

00:15:03.578 --> 00:15:06.320
This is a trouser waistband
that's got a built-in

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.103
hook and eye closure.

00:15:08.103 --> 00:15:10.370
If you do lose a little bit of weight,

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:13.180
you can actually cinch
this and it'll take it in

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:15.130
three-quarters of an inch on each side.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:19.690
Once the sewing process is done,

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:21.850
it goes to one of the
hand-finishing tailors.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:25.520
There's a lot of hand
work, even on a track suit,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:28.860
like the hem on these
trousers is done by hand.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:30.790
There's an insane amount of hand finishing

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:33.590
on the insides of the
jackets, because it's unlined.

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:35.390
So when you open it, it's beautiful.

00:15:40.574 --> 00:15:41.407
Hey, man.
Hey, what's good?

00:15:41.407 --> 00:15:42.860
What's good with you, man?

00:15:44.485 --> 00:15:46.402
Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.410
Once I'm presenting
the suit to the client,

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:51.840
you know, it's a full on presentation.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:54.540
So it's a very minimal garment bag.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.840
You don't know what to expect.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:57.810
I kind of like that element of surprise.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:15:58.980
You didn't see it at all, did you?

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.823
No.
Great, great, great.

00:16:00.823 --> 00:16:02.233
Remember what we discussed?
Yes.

00:16:02.233 --> 00:16:03.662
I'm from Memphis.

00:16:03.662 --> 00:16:05.305
I'm gonna say something slick.

00:16:05.305 --> 00:16:06.777
What did we discuss?

00:16:06.777 --> 00:16:10.848
Navy blue and black.
Ooh, and then what else?

00:16:10.848 --> 00:16:12.516
A little olive.
Ooh!

00:16:12.516 --> 00:16:13.349
Is that true?

00:16:13.349 --> 00:16:14.182
Is that what you're thinking?

00:16:14.182 --> 00:16:15.015
And then I'm gonna open it,

00:16:15.015 --> 00:16:17.220
and the client's usually gonna scream.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:18.077
Oof!

00:16:18.077 --> 00:16:21.750
Oh, man! I see some fire.

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.493
Or exhale real heavily.

00:16:23.493 --> 00:16:25.629
There's always a moment of drama,

00:16:25.629 --> 00:16:29.021
every time I unzip and fold it back.

00:16:29.021 --> 00:16:31.860
Oh, goodness!
It was my chance.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.014
Bro!
My chance.

00:16:33.014 --> 00:16:34.279
Bro!

00:16:34.279 --> 00:16:35.690
something like that?

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:36.850
Man!
Put these on,

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.440
and when you come back,
I'll put this on you.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:40.393
All right, bet.

00:16:40.393 --> 00:16:42.911
So I let them have that moment.

00:16:42.911 --> 00:16:44.322
Oh, a hundred percent.

00:16:44.322 --> 00:16:45.905
Wow!
100%.

00:16:47.733 --> 00:16:50.619
Like, you don't even
have to think about it.

00:16:50.619 --> 00:16:52.090
Oh!
Right?

00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:54.290
I'm gonna make sure that everything fits

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.910
the way it's supposed to.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.880
And again, if anything needs
to be modified, now's the time.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:02.330
But if not, we shake hands,

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:03.767
we hug it out and they take it with them.

00:17:03.767 --> 00:17:06.157
I noticed it, but I'm
just taking all of it.

00:17:06.157 --> 00:17:07.803
I'm taking all of it in.

00:17:09.641 --> 00:17:13.250
My hope is that they will be given more

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:16.233
and more opportunities
to flex their skills,

00:17:16.233 --> 00:17:19.967
to show how dynamic they are

00:17:19.967 --> 00:17:23.980
and what new, fresh elements
of art they can bring

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.913
to the fashion industry.

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:27.820
Well, it's kind of like

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:30.870
a "Pursuit of Happyness"
story almost, you know?

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.633
And like that was one
of his favorite movies.

00:17:32.633 --> 00:17:33.500
I'm not gonna lie.

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:35.250
He watched it so much he
used to get on my nerves.

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:37.620
It became part of his story.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:39.870
But one of the most important
things I could take away

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:42.801
on an entrepreneurial
level and as a businessman

00:17:42.801 --> 00:17:46.395
is just dream big and go after your goals.

00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:48.090
The only one that can stop you

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.174
from becoming successful is yourself.

00:17:50.174 --> 00:17:52.230
When I started, I had no idea

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.943
it was going to turn into this.

00:17:54.943 --> 00:17:56.962
It's a tracksuit empire.

00:17:56.962 --> 00:17:58.713
I don't have any investors.

00:17:58.713 --> 00:18:00.370
All this is all money in.

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:02.180
This is me going out and hustling,

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.360
bringing that money back
and just building it,

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:06.003
building it, building it.

00:18:06.003 --> 00:18:08.820
There's so much more beyond this guy.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:10.710
I was really no telling
what he would come up with,

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.275
but we do know whatever he comes up with.

00:18:12.275 --> 00:18:13.427
It's going to be dope.

00:18:13.427 --> 00:18:16.292
So yeah, there's really, there's no limit.

00:18:16.292 --> 00:18:17.979
You can't put him in a box.

00:18:17.979 --> 00:18:20.300
You can't put a sky over him,

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.000
'cause he's gonna bust through it.

